LS.add({"a77170e3bd":{"t":"聖潔生活系列 Holy Living Series","b":4,"k":"folder","h":"<nav class=\"cr\" style=\"--c:#2f7fd8\"><a href=\"index.html\">燈臺 <i lang=\"en\">The Lampstand</i></a> <span class=\"sep\">›</span> <a class=\"fl0\" href=\"#i9ee97a219f\">4 講道 Sermons</a> <span class=\"sep\">›</span> <a class=\"fl1\" href=\"#i73f4e1820c\">講道系列 Preaching Series</a></nav><h1 class=\"fl2\">聖潔生活系列 Holy Living Series</h1><ul class=\"ls\"><li class=\"ln dn fl3\" data-m=\"2024-10-27\"><a href=\"#i43ce913e22\">2024-10-27 抵擋魔鬼</a></li><li class=\"ln dn fl3\" data-m=\"2024-09-08\"><a href=\"#i776443be50\">2024-09-08 被聖靈充滿</a></li><li class=\"ln dn fl3\" data-m=\"2024-08-04\"><a href=\"#ieac183427e\">2024-08-04 敬畏神</a></li></ul><details class=\"pn across\" open><summary>跨燈 <i lang=\"en\">Across the lamps</i></summary><p class=\"acr\" data-b=\"4\"></p></details>"},"a7cb7120fd":{"t":"蒂莉・史密斯與南亞海嘯 （Tilly Smith 2004）","b":8,"k":"unit","h":"<nav class=\"cr\" style=\"--c:#00a3a3\"><a href=\"index.html\">燈臺 <i lang=\"en\">The Lampstand</i></a> <span class=\"sep\">›</span> <a class=\"fl0\" href=\"#if7f623b9fc\">8 世界觀察 World Watch</a> <span class=\"sep\">›</span> <a class=\"fl1\" href=\"#i7790881d9e\">5 事件 Events</a> <span class=\"sep\">›</span> <a class=\"fl2\" href=\"#ic2c2efd310\">5.1 災難與悲劇 Disasters &amp; tragedies</a></nav><h1 class=\"fl3\">蒂莉・史密斯與南亞海嘯 （Tilly Smith 2004）</h1><p class=\"og\">HTML · 6 KB</p><div class=\"topopen\"><a class=\"opn\" href=\"world-watch/cases/tilly-smith-2004.html\">打開 <i lang=\"en\">Open</i></a></div><details class=\"pn across\" open><summary>跨燈 <i lang=\"en\">Across the lamps</i></summary><p class=\"acr\" data-b=\"8\"></p></details>"},"a77c471362":{"t":"S30 sons of God 神的眾子","b":1,"k":"unit","h":"<nav class=\"cr\" style=\"--c:#c0841a\"><a href=\"index.html\">燈臺 <i lang=\"en\">The Lampstand</i></a> <span class=\"sep\">›</span> <a class=\"fl0\" href=\"#ic52d1cf364\">1 原文研究 Original Languages</a> <span class=\"sep\">›</span> <a class=\"fl1\" href=\"#ie883cc6886\">1 詞彙 Vocabulary</a> <span class=\"sep\">›</span> <a class=\"fl2\" href=\"#i52badd311e\">Studies 研究</a></nav><h1 class=\"fl3\" id=\"s30--神的眾子-sons-of-god\">S30 · <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">神的眾子</span> sons of God</h1><details class=\"toc\"><summary>目錄 <i lang=\"en\">Contents</i></summary><ol><li class=\"l2\"><a href=\"#1-at-a-glance-%E4%B8%80%E8%A6%BD\">1. At a glance 一覽</a></li><li class=\"l2\"><a href=\"#2-root-and-family-%E5%AD%97%E6%A0%B9%E8%88%87%E5%AD%97%E6%97%8F\">2. Root and family 字根與字族</a></li><li class=\"l2\"><a href=\"#3-counts-and-significance-%E6%95%B8%E6%93%9A%E8%88%87%E6%84%8F%E7%BE%A9\">3. Counts and significance 數據與意義</a></li><li class=\"l3\"><a href=\"#1-totals-%E7%B8%BD%E6%95%B8\">1. Totals 總數</a></li><li class=\"l3\"><a href=\"#2-by-book-%E6%8C%89%E6%9B%B8%E5%8D%B7\">2. By book 按書卷</a></li><li class=\"l3\"><a href=\"#3-by-section-%E6%8C%89%E5%88%86%E6%AE%B5\">3. By section 按分段</a></li><li class=\"l3\"><a href=\"#4-by-grammatical-form-%E6%8C%89%E8%A9%9E%E5%BD%A2\">4. By grammatical form 按詞形</a></li><li class=\"l3\"><a href=\"#5-by-translation-%E6%8C%89%E8%AD%AF%E6%9C%AC\">5. By translation 按譯本</a></li><li class=\"l3\"><a href=\"#6-first-and-last-use-rank-hapax-%E9%A6%96%E6%AC%A1%E8%88%87%E6%9C%AB%E6%AC%A1%E6%8E%92%E5%90%8D%E7%BD%95%E7%94%A8\">6. First and last use; rank; hapax 首次與末次、排名、罕用</a></li><li class=\"l3\"><a href=\"#7-spread-%E5%88%86%E4%BD%88\">7. Spread 分佈</a></li><li class=\"l3\"><a href=\"#8-significance-%E6%84%8F%E7%BE%A9\">8. Significance 意義</a></li><li class=\"l2\"><a href=\"#4-%E5%92%8C%E5%90%88%E6%9C%AC-renderings-%E5%92%8C%E5%90%88%E6%9C%AC%E7%9A%84%E8%AD%AF%E6%B3%95-every-rendering-every-verse\">4. 和合本 renderings 和合本的譯法 (every rendering, every verse)</a></li><li class=\"l2\"><a href=\"#5-shades-of-meaning-%E6%84%8F%E7%BE%A9%E5%B1%A4%E6%AC%A1\">5. Shades of meaning 意義層次</a></li><li class=\"l2\"><a href=\"#6-the-lxx-bridge-%E4%B8%83%E5%8D%81%E5%A3%AB%E8%AD%AF%E6%9C%AC%E7%9A%84%E6%A9%8B%E6%A8%91\">6. The LXX bridge 七十士譯本的橋樑</a></li><li class=\"l2\"><a href=\"#7-companions-and-cross-references-%E5%90%8C%E4%BC%B4%E5%AD%97%E8%88%87%E4%BA%92%E5%8F%83\">7. Companions and cross-references 同伴字與互參</a></li><li class=\"l2\"><a href=\"#8-in-the-new-testament-%E6%96%B0%E7%B4%84%E7%9A%84%E7%94%A8%E6%B3%95\">8. In the New Testament 新約的用法</a></li><li class=\"l2\"><a href=\"#9-culture-and-background-%E6%96%87%E5%8C%96%E8%83%8C%E6%99%AF\">9. Culture and background 文化背景</a></li><li class=\"l2\"><a href=\"#10-theology-%E7%A5%9E%E5%AD%B8\">10. Theology 神學</a></li><li class=\"l2\"><a href=\"#12-for-practice-%E6%87%89%E7%94%A8\">11. For practice 應用</a></li><li class=\"l2\"><a href=\"#13-evaluation-%E8%A9%95%E4%BC%B0\">12. Evaluation 評估</a></li><li class=\"l2\"><a href=\"#14-sources-and-to-check-%E8%B3%87%E6%96%99%E8%88%87%E5%BE%85%E6%9F%A5\">13. Sources 資料</a></li></ol></details>\n<p><span class=\"off\">Angels <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">天使論</span></span> · <span class=\"off\">Salvation <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">救恩論</span></span> · <span class=\"off\">Jesus Christ <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">基督論</span></span><span class=\"off\"></span></p>\n<h2 id=\"1-at-a-glance-一覽\">1. At a glance <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">一覽</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Hebrew:</strong> <big><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בַּר</span></big> <em>bar</em> <span class=\"off\">H1247</span> · <big><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">נְפִיל</span></big> <em>nᵉphîyl</em> <span class=\"off\">H5303</span></p>\n<p><strong>Greek:</strong> <big><span lang=\"grc\">υἱοθεσία</span></big> <em>huiothesía</em> <span class=\"off\">G5206</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A phrase, not a word.</strong> The noun <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בֵּן</span> stands 4,946 times in 3,662 verses, and almost all of that lies outside this study. Its plain senses are set out below (section 2). The subject here is one phrase and its near relatives: <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בְּנֵי הָאֱלֹהִים</span> four times, <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בְּנֵי אֱלֹהִים</span> once, <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בְּנֵי אֵלִים</span> twice, and the Aramaic of <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.3.25\">Dan 3:25</span> once. That is eight places in all, seven Hebrew and one Aramaic. The word families in section 3 are therefore only the small words that belong beside the phrase (the Aramaic <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בַּר</span>, the Nephilim of <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.6.4\">Gen 6:4</span> and the Greek <span lang=\"grc\">υἱοθεσία</span>).</li>\n<li><strong>In the heavenly court.</strong> \"Now it came to pass on the day when the sons of God came to present themselves before Jehovah, that Satan also came among them.\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.1.6\">Job 1:6</span>, ASV) (<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בְּנֵ֣י הָ אֱלֹהִ֔ים</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.1.6\">Job 1:6</span>, BHSA)). The scene returns at <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.2.1\">Job 2:1</span>. In <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.38.7\">Job 38:7</span> they are present at the founding of the earth: \"When the morning stars sang together, And all the sons of God shouted for joy?\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.38.7\">Job 38:7</span>, ASV) (<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">כָּל־ בְּנֵ֥י אֱלֹהִֽים׃</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.38.7\">Job 38:7</span>, BHSA)).</li>\n<li><strong>The disputed passage.</strong> \"that the sons of God saw the daughters of men that they were fair; and they took them wives of all that they chose.\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.6.2\">Gen 6:2</span>, ASV) (<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בְנֵי־ הָֽ אֱלֹהִים֙</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.6.2\">Gen 6:2</span>, BHSA)). The phrase appears again at <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.6.4\">Gen 6:4</span>, beside the Nephilim. Three classic readings have been held (angels, the line of Seth, rulers), and section 10 sets them out. The first two occurrences in canonical order are the disputed ones.</li>\n<li><strong>Called to worship.</strong> \"Ascribe unto Jehovah, O ye sons of the mighty,\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.29.1\">Ps 29:1</span>, ASV) (<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בְּנֵ֣י אֵלִ֑ים</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.29.1\">Ps 29:1</span>, BHSA)), and \"Who among the sons of the mighty is like unto Jehovah,\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.89.6\">Ps 89:6</span>, ASV) (<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בִּ בְנֵ֥י אֵלִים׃</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.89.6\">Ps 89:6</span>, BHSA)). The Hebrew has the shorter word for God in the plural. The ASV and the KJV differ from the <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本</span> in how they read it (section 4).</li>\n<li><strong>Near relatives, used of people.</strong> The expression \"sons of the Most High\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.82.6\">Ps 82:6</span>, ASV) (<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">וּ בְנֵ֖י עֶלְיֹ֣ון כֻּלְּכֶֽם׃</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.82.6\">Ps 82:6</span>, BHSA)) is addressed to figures who are told they will die, and who they are is debated (section 10). \"Ye are the sons of the living God.\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Hos.1.10\">Hos 1:10</span>, ASV) (<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בְּנֵ֥י אֵֽל־ חָֽי׃</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Hos.1.10\">Hos 1:10</span>, BHSA)) is said of Israel. Israel is also God's son in the singular: \"Israel is my son, my first-born:\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Exod.4.22\">Exod 4:22</span>, ASV) (<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בְּנִ֥י בְכֹרִ֖י יִשְׂרָאֵֽל׃</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Exod.4.22\">Exod 4:22</span>, BHSA)).</li>\n<li><strong>One Aramaic line, in a Babylonian king's mouth.</strong> \"and the aspect of the fourth is like a son of the gods.\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.3.25\">Dan 3:25</span>, ASV) (<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">לְ בַר־ אֱלָהִֽין׃</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.3.25\">Dan 3:25</span>, BHSA)). The noun for son is singular and the word for gods is plural, in the absolute state. The KJV prints \"the Son of God\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.3.25\">Dan 3:25</span>, KJV), the ASV \"a son of the gods\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.3.25\">Dan 3:25</span>, ASV), and the <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本 「好像神子」（</span><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.3.25\">Dan 3:25</span><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">，和合本）</span>. What the king meant is taken up in section 10.</li>\n<li><strong>A verse where the manuscripts part.</strong> At <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Deut.32.8\">Deut 32:8</span> the Masoretic Text has <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">לְ מִסְפַּ֖ר בְּנֵ֥י יִשְׂרָאֵֽל׃</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Deut.32.8\">Deut 32:8</span>, BHSA). The Greek used here has <em><span lang=\"grc\">κατά αριθμόν αγγέλων θεού</span></em> (LXX <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Deut.32.8\">Deut 32:8</span>, ABP), and a Qumran copy (4Q37) has sons of God at this place (section 10).</li>\n<li><strong>The New Testament turns the phrase toward people.</strong> The plural <span lang=\"grc\">υἱοὶ Θεοῦ</span> stands in six verses, all of people: <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Matt.5.9\">Matt 5:9</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.20.36\">Luke 20:36</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.8.14\">Rom 8:14</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.8.19\">19</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.9.26\">9:26</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gal.3.26\">Gal 3:26</span>. <span lang=\"grc\">τέκνα Θεοῦ</span> stands in ten more (section 8). No New Testament verse applies the plural phrase to angels. Hebrews asks \"For unto which of the angels said he at any time, Thou art my Son\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Heb.1.5\">Heb 1:5</span>, ASV). The sonship of believers is given, through Christ, by the Spirit and by faith.</li>\n<li><strong>What is not said.</strong> No verse names any one of the sons of God, or gives their number, or says what they look like. No verse outside Gen 6 applies the phrase <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בְּנֵי הָאֱלֹהִים</span> to the descendants of Seth or to rulers. Whether Gen 6 uses it as Job does is the question.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">看圖</span> See it</strong> — the counts by book and by section are the two charts of section 3; the renderings with their counts are sections 3.7 and 4:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Bible circles <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">聖經圓圖</span>: <span class=\"off\">Genesis 6, before the flood</span> · <span class=\"off\">Job 1, the court</span> · <span class=\"off\">Psalm 82, the gods who die</span> · <span class=\"off\">Daniel 3, the furnace</span> · <span class=\"off\">Romans 8, the sons of God</span></li>\n<li>People (the circles' people, four languages): <span class=\"off\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">挪亞</span> Noah <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">נֹחַ</span> <span lang=\"grc\">Νῶε</span></span> (Gen 6) · <span class=\"off\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">塞特</span> Seth <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שֵׁת</span> <span lang=\"grc\">Σήθ</span></span> (the line of Seth) · <span class=\"off\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">以諾</span> Enoch <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">חֲנוֹךְ</span> <span lang=\"grc\">Ἑνώχ</span></span> (1 Enoch) · <span class=\"off\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">約伯</span> Job <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">אִיּוֹב</span> <span lang=\"grc\">Ἰώβ</span></span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.1.6\">Job 1:6</span>) · <span class=\"off\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">尼布甲尼撒</span> Nebuchadnezzar <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">נְבוּכַדְנֶאצַּר</span></span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.3.25\">Dan 3:25</span>) · <span class=\"off\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">何西阿</span> Hosea <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">הוֹשֵׁעַ</span> <span lang=\"grc\">Ὡσηέ</span></span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Hos.1.10\">Hos 1:10</span>) · <span class=\"off\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">彼得</span> Peter <span lang=\"grc\">Πέτρος</span></span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"1Pet.3.19\">1 Pet 3:19</span>) · <span class=\"off\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">猶大</span> Jude <span lang=\"grc\">Ἰούδας</span></span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Jude.1.6\">Jude 6</span>). The circles have no row for the sons of God themselves.</li>\n<li>Places: <span class=\"off\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">黑門</span> Hermon <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">חֶרְמוֹן</span></span> (1 Enoch 6) · <span class=\"off\">the Holy Land in 3D <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">聖地立體地圖</span></span> (the map has no place links yet: find them by name)</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote class=\"verse\"><div class=\"zh\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">神的兒子們看見人的女子美貌，就隨意挑選，娶來為妻。（<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.6.2\">創世記 6:2</span>）</span></div><div class=\"en\">that the sons of God saw the daughters of men that they were fair; and they took them wives of all that they chose. (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.6.2\">Genesis 6:2</span>, ASV)</div></blockquote>\n<blockquote class=\"verse\"><div class=\"zh\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">有一天，神的眾子來侍立在耶和華面前，撒但也來在其中。（<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.1.6\">約伯記 1:6</span>）</span></div><div class=\"en\">Now it came to pass on the day when the sons of God came to present themselves before Jehovah, that Satan also came among them. (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.1.6\">Job 1:6</span>, ASV)</div></blockquote>\n<blockquote class=\"verse\"><div class=\"zh\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">那時，晨星一同歌唱；神的眾子也都歡呼。（<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.38.7\">約伯記 38:7</span>）</span></div><div class=\"en\">When the morning stars sang together, And all the sons of God shouted for joy? (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.38.7\">Job 38:7</span>, ASV)</div></blockquote>\n<blockquote class=\"verse\"><div class=\"zh\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">神的眾子啊，你們要將榮耀、能力歸給耶和華，歸給耶和華！（<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.29.1\">詩篇 29:1</span>）</span></div><div class=\"en\">Ascribe unto Jehovah, O ye sons of the mighty, Ascribe unto Jehovah glory and strength. (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.29.1\">Psalm 29:1</span>, ASV)</div></blockquote>\n<blockquote class=\"verse\"><div class=\"zh\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">王說：「看哪，我見有四個人，並沒有捆綁，在火中遊行，也沒有受傷；那第四個的相貌好像神子。」（<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.3.25\">但以理書 3:25</span>）</span></div><div class=\"en\">He answered and said, Lo, I see four men loose, walking in the midst of the fire, and they have no hurt; and the aspect of the fourth is like a son of the gods. (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.3.25\">Daniel 3:25</span>, ASV)</div></blockquote>\n<blockquote class=\"verse\"><div class=\"zh\"><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">因為凡被神的靈引導的，都是神的兒子。（<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.8.14\">羅馬書 8:14</span>）</span></div><div class=\"en\">For as many as are led by the Spirit of God, these are sons of God. (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.8.14\">Romans 8:14</span>, ASV)</div></blockquote>\n<h2 id=\"2-root-and-family-字根與字族\">2. Root and family <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">字根與字族</span> <a class=\"up\" href=\"#1-at-a-glance-%E4%B8%80%E8%A6%BD\" title=\"回到摘要 back to the summary\">↑</a></h2>\n<p><strong>Compounds in this study (Strong's data):</strong> <span lang=\"grc\">υἱοθεσία</span> <em>huiothesía</em> <span class=\"off\">G5206</span> = <span lang=\"grc\">υἱός</span> <em>huiós</em> <span class=\"off\">G5207</span> + <span lang=\"grc\">τίθημι</span> <em>títhēmi</em> <span class=\"off\">G5087</span>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בֵּן</span> (H1121): Strong's 'from <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בָּנָה</span> H1129', with 'son, grandson, child, member of a group'. It stands 4,946 times in 3,662 verses, most in 1 Chronicles (706), Numbers (611) and Genesis (365). This study does not follow it at large.</li>\n<li>Plain senses of the noun, counted. Of the forms of <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בֵּן</span> in the construct state with a following word (3,720), the commonest following words are <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">יִשְׂרָאֵל</span> (640 times), <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">אָדָם</span> (155), <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">עַמֹּון</span> (104) and <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">אַהֲרֹן</span> (64). So the phrase can mean literally a child, a member of a people or a family, a human being (<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בֶּן־אָדָם</span>), or an age, as in <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׁנָה</span> (53 times, a son of so many years). It is also said of animals: <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בָּקָר</span> (40 times, a son of the herd). The idiom names what someone or something belongs to.</li>\n<li>The idiom and this phrase. ISBE (James Crichton) says the heavenly beings are so called 'simply as belonging to the same class or guild as the' Elohim, just as the sons of the prophets belong to the prophets' order (ISBE citing A. B. Davidson on <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.1.6\">Job 1:6</span>). If that is right, the phrase names a class and says nothing about descent. Keil also rules out descent: the notion that the title presupposes physical generation, he says, 'we must reject as an unscriptural and gnostic error'.</li>\n<li>The phrases (BHSA; in every case the first noun is plural construct and the second is absolute). <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בְּנֵי הָאֱלֹהִים</span> with the article: 4 words in 4 verses, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.6.2\">Gen 6:2</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.6.4\">6:4</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.1.6\">Job 1:6</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.2.1\">2:1</span>. <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בְּנֵי אֱלֹהִים</span> without the article: 1, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.38.7\">Job 38:7</span>. <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בְּנֵי אֵלִים</span>: 2, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.29.1\">Ps 29:1</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.89.6\">89:6</span> (numbered 89:7 in the Hebrew, ISBE says, and 88:7 in the Greek). <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בְנֵי עֶלְיֹון</span>: 1, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.82.6\">Ps 82:6</span>. <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בְּנֵי אֵל־חָי</span>: 1, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Hos.1.10\">Hos 1:10</span>.</li>\n<li>The Aramaic. <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בַּר</span> (H1247) stands 8 times in 7 verses, four in Ezra and four in Daniel. <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.3.25\">Dan 3:25</span> is the one place where it stands with the plural of <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">אֱלָהּ</span> (H426). The same noun stands at Dan 7:13, <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">כְּ בַ֥ר אֱנָ֖שׁ</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.7.13\">Dan 7:13</span>, BHSA). BDB glosses the phrase at Dan 3:25 'a divine (or angelic) being' and compares <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.6.2\">Gen 6:2</span> and <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.1.6\">Job 1:6</span>.</li>\n<li>Nephilim. <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">נְפִיל</span> (H5303, in the plural) stands 3 times in 2 verses: <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.6.4\">Gen 6:4</span> and <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.13.33\">Num 13:33</span> (twice). Strong's has 'from <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">נָפַל</span> H5307' and 'giants, the Nephilim'. BDB glosses the word by the Greek <span lang=\"grc\">γίγαντες</span> and says 'etymology dubious'.</li>\n<li><span lang=\"grc\">υἱοθεσία</span> (G5206) stands 5 times: <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.8.15\">Rom 8:15</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.8.23\">23</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.9.4\">9:4</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gal.4.5\">Gal 4:5</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Eph.1.5\">Eph 1:5</span>. Strong's calls it 'the placing as a son, i.e. adoption (figuratively, Christian sonship in respect to God)'.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"3-counts-and-significance-數據與意義\">3. Counts and significance <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">數據與意義</span> <a class=\"up\" href=\"#1-at-a-glance-%E4%B8%80%E8%A6%BD\" title=\"回到摘要 back to the summary\">↑</a></h2>\n<p><em>Sources: OT = BHSA (ETCBC words, prefixes count as words); NT = OpenGNT 3.3; KJV and ASV = eBible texts with Strong's tags; <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本</span> = the word-by-word alignments (OpenHebrewBible, OpenGNT). Tests: log-likelihood G² with 1 degree of freedom.</em></p>\n<h3 id=\"1-totals-總數\">1. Totals <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">總數</span></h3>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Lemma</th><th>Testament</th><th>Words</th><th>Verses</th><th>Forms</th><th>Commonest forms</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בַּר</span> <em>bar</em> <span class=\"off\">H1247</span></td><td>OT</td><td>8</td><td>7</td><td>4</td><td><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בַּר־</span> 3, <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בַר־</span> 2, <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בַ֥ר</span> 2</td></tr><tr><td><strong>sons-of-God Aramaic <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בַּר</span> family</strong></td><td>OT</td><td><strong>8</strong></td><td><strong>7</strong></td><td>4</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">נְפִיל</span> <em>nᵉphîyl</em> <span class=\"off\">H5303</span></td><td>OT</td><td>3</td><td>2</td><td>3</td><td><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">נְּפִלִ֞ים</span> 1, <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">נְּפִילִ֛ים</span> 1, <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">נְּפִלִ֑ים</span> 1</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hebrew Nephilim <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">נְפִיל</span> family</strong></td><td>OT</td><td><strong>3</strong></td><td><strong>2</strong></td><td>3</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"grc\">υἱοθεσία</span> <em>huiothesía</em> <span class=\"off\">G5206</span></td><td>NT</td><td>5</td><td>5</td><td>3</td><td><span lang=\"grc\">υἱοθεσίαν</span> 3, <span lang=\"grc\">υἱοθεσίας</span> 1, <span lang=\"grc\">υἱοθεσία</span> 1</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Greek adoption <span lang=\"grc\">υἱοθεσία</span> family</strong></td><td>NT</td><td><strong>5</strong></td><td><strong>5</strong></td><td>3</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Whole Bible:</strong> 11 Hebrew words + 5 Greek words = 16 (the two languages are counted in their own texts; the Septuagint is not added).</p>\n<p><strong>Lexeme check (Strong's tag against the BHSA lexeme):</strong> every word counted carries the same BHSA lexeme as its Strong's number.</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-by-book-按書卷\">2. By book <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">按書卷</span></h3>\n<p><img class=\"emb\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"charts/S30 sons of God - books.svg\" src=\"pages/f79a8be416.svg\"></p>\n<p><strong>sons-of-God Aramaic <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בַּר</span>.</strong> Most uses: Ezra 4, Dan 4. Highest rate (2 or more uses): Ezra 7.6, Dan 5.0.</p>\n<p><strong>Keyness:</strong> Ezra (G² 24, p &lt; 0.0001, 40.0× expected), Dan (G² 21, p &lt; 0.0001, 20.0× expected).</p>\n<details class=\"callout c-table\"><summary class=\"ctitle\">sons-of-God Aramaic <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בַּר</span>: every book (count, rate, expected, G²)</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Book</th><th>Count</th><th>Per 10,000</th><th>Expected</th><th>G²</th><th>p</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ezra <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">以斯拉記</span></td><td>4</td><td>7.59</td><td>0.1</td><td>24.2</td><td>p &lt; 0.0001</td></tr><tr><td>Dan <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">但以理書</span></td><td>4</td><td>4.96</td><td>0.2</td><td>20.8</td><td>p &lt; 0.0001</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></details>\n<p><strong>Hebrew Nephilim <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">נְפִיל</span>.</strong> Most uses: Num 2, Gen 1. Highest rate (2 or more uses): Num 0.9.</p>\n<p><strong>Keyness:</strong> no book stands out at p &lt; 0.001.</p>\n<details class=\"callout c-table\"><summary class=\"ctitle\">Hebrew Nephilim <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">נְפִיל</span>: every book (count, rate, expected, G²)</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Book</th><th>Count</th><th>Per 10,000</th><th>Expected</th><th>G²</th><th>p</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Gen <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">創世記</span></td><td>1</td><td>0.35</td><td>0.2</td><td>1.9</td><td>not significant</td></tr><tr><td>Num <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">民數記</span></td><td>2</td><td>0.86</td><td>0.2</td><td>7.9</td><td>p &lt; 0.01</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></details>\n<p><strong>Greek adoption <span lang=\"grc\">υἱοθεσία</span>.</strong> Most uses: Rom 3, Gal 1, Eph 1. Highest rate (2 or more uses): Rom 4.2.</p>\n<p><strong>Keyness:</strong> Rom (G² 11, p &lt; 0.001, 10.0× expected).</p>\n<details class=\"callout c-table\"><summary class=\"ctitle\">Greek adoption <span lang=\"grc\">υἱοθεσία</span>: every book (count, rate, expected, G²)</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Book</th><th>Count</th><th>Per 10,000</th><th>Expected</th><th>G²</th><th>p</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Rom <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">羅馬書</span></td><td>3</td><td>4.22</td><td>0.3</td><td>11.3</td><td>p &lt; 0.001</td></tr><tr><td>Gal <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">加拉太書</span></td><td>1</td><td>4.48</td><td>0.1</td><td>3.4</td><td>not significant</td></tr><tr><td>Eph <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">以弗所書</span></td><td>1</td><td>4.13</td><td>0.1</td><td>3.2</td><td>not significant</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></details>\n<p><em>G² (log-likelihood) compares the count in a book with what the book's size predicts; G² ≥ 10.83 means p &lt; 0.001, a chance below 1 in 1,000 that the excess is luck.</em></p>\n<h3 id=\"3-by-section-按分段\">3. By section <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">按分段</span></h3>\n<p><img class=\"emb\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"charts/S30 sons of God - sections.svg\" src=\"pages/f11292bf39.svg\"></p>\n<p><strong>sons-of-God Aramaic <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בַּר</span></strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Count</th><th>Per 10,000</th><th>Expected</th><th>G²</th><th>p</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Law <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">律法書</span></td><td>0</td><td>0.00</td><td>2.1</td><td>4.9</td><td>below</td></tr><tr><td>History <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">歷史書</span></td><td>4</td><td>0.26</td><td>2.9</td><td>0.6</td><td>not significant</td></tr><tr><td>Wisdom and Poetry <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">詩歌智慧書</span></td><td>0</td><td>0.00</td><td>1.0</td><td>2.0</td><td>below</td></tr><tr><td>Major Prophets <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">大先知書</span></td><td>4</td><td>0.45</td><td>1.7</td><td>3.3</td><td>not significant</td></tr><tr><td>Minor Prophets <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">小先知書</span></td><td>0</td><td>0.00</td><td>0.4</td><td>0.8</td><td>below</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Hebrew Nephilim <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">נְפִיל</span></strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Count</th><th>Per 10,000</th><th>Expected</th><th>G²</th><th>p</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Law <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">律法書</span></td><td>3</td><td>0.27</td><td>0.8</td><td>8.0</td><td>p &lt; 0.01</td></tr><tr><td>History <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">歷史書</span></td><td>0</td><td>0.00</td><td>1.1</td><td>2.7</td><td>below</td></tr><tr><td>Wisdom and Poetry <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">詩歌智慧書</span></td><td>0</td><td>0.00</td><td>0.4</td><td>0.8</td><td>below</td></tr><tr><td>Major Prophets <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">大先知書</span></td><td>0</td><td>0.00</td><td>0.6</td><td>1.4</td><td>below</td></tr><tr><td>Minor Prophets <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">小先知書</span></td><td>0</td><td>0.00</td><td>0.1</td><td>0.3</td><td>below</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Greek adoption <span lang=\"grc\">υἱοθεσία</span></strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Count</th><th>Per 10,000</th><th>Expected</th><th>G²</th><th>p</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Gospels <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">福音書</span></td><td>0</td><td>0.00</td><td>2.3</td><td>6.3</td><td>below</td></tr><tr><td>Acts <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">使徒行傳</span></td><td>0</td><td>0.00</td><td>0.7</td><td>1.4</td><td>below</td></tr><tr><td>Paul's letters <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">保羅書信</span></td><td>5</td><td>1.54</td><td>1.2</td><td>14.5</td><td>p &lt; 0.001</td></tr><tr><td>Hebrews and the General letters <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">希伯來書與普通書信</span></td><td>0</td><td>0.00</td><td>0.5</td><td>1.0</td><td>below</td></tr><tr><td>Revelation <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">啟示錄</span></td><td>0</td><td>0.00</td><td>0.4</td><td>0.7</td><td>below</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"4-by-grammatical-form-按詞形\">4. By grammatical form <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">按詞形</span></h3>\n<ul>\n<li><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בַּר</span> <em>bar</em> <span class=\"off\">H1247</span> — <em>part of speech:</em> noun 8 · <em>number:</em> singular 8 · <em>state:</em> construct 7, absolute 1 · <em>pronoun suffix:</em> none 7, his / her / their 1</li>\n<li><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">נְפִיל</span> <em>nᵉphîyl</em> <span class=\"off\">H5303</span> — <em>part of speech:</em> noun 3 · <em>number:</em> plural 3 · <em>state:</em> absolute 3 · <em>pronoun suffix:</em> none 3</li>\n<li><span lang=\"grc\">υἱοθεσία</span> <em>huiothesía</em> <span class=\"off\">G5206</span> — <em>part of speech:</em> noun 5 · <em>case:</em> accusative 3, genitive 1, nominative 1 · <em>number:</em> singular 5</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"5-by-translation-按譯本\">5. By translation <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">按譯本</span></h3>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Lemma</th><th>KJV (its own tags)</th><th>ASV (machine-tagged)</th><th><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本</span> (alignment; full list in section 4)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בַּר</span> <em>bar</em> <span class=\"off\">H1247</span></td><td>son 7, old 1</td><td>son 4, son of 3</td><td><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">兒子 3, 孫子</span> 2</td></tr><tr><td><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">נְפִיל</span> <em>nᵉphîyl</em> <span class=\"off\">H5303</span></td><td>giants 3</td><td>Nephilim 3</td><td><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">偉人</span> 3</td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"grc\">υἱοθεσία</span> <em>huiothesía</em> <span class=\"off\">G5206</span></td><td>adoption 3, sons 1, children 1</td><td>adoption 5, sons 2</td><td><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">兒子的名分 4, 兒子</span> 1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><em>The KJV counts are its own Strong's tags. The ASV's tags were added by machine and sometimes sit on the wrong word, so its counts are a guide only; \"–\" = no tag found. Small words that carry a tag (and, of, the, for, let …) are left out of both lists.</em></p>\n<h3 id=\"6-first-and-last-use-rank-hapax-首次與末次排名罕用\">6. First and last use; rank; hapax <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">首次與末次、排名、罕用</span></h3>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Lemma</th><th>First</th><th>Last</th><th>Rank among all lemmas</th><th>Once only?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בַּר</span> <em>bar</em> <span class=\"off\">H1247</span></td><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ezra.5.1\">Ezra 5:1</span></td><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.7.13\">Dan 7:13</span></td><td>2590 of 8,563 (OT)</td><td>no</td></tr><tr><td><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">נְפִיל</span> <em>nᵉphîyl</em> <span class=\"off\">H5303</span></td><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.6.4\">Gen 6:4</span></td><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.13.33\">Num 13:33</span></td><td>4096 of 8,563 (OT)</td><td>no</td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"grc\">υἱοθεσία</span> <em>huiothesía</em> <span class=\"off\">G5206</span></td><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.8.15\">Rom 8:15</span></td><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Eph.1.5\">Eph 1:5</span></td><td>1637 of 5,399 (NT)</td><td>no</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"7-spread-分佈\">7. Spread <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">分佈</span></h3>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Lemma / family</th><th>Books with it</th><th>DP (0 = even, 1 = one corner)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בַּר</span> <em>bar</em> <span class=\"off\">H1247</span></td><td>2 of 39</td><td>0.969</td></tr><tr><td><strong>sons-of-God Aramaic <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בַּר</span> family</strong></td><td>2 of 39</td><td><strong>0.969</strong></td></tr><tr><td><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">נְפִיל</span> <em>nᵉphîyl</em> <span class=\"off\">H5303</span></td><td>2 of 39</td><td>0.878</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hebrew Nephilim <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">נְפִיל</span> family</strong></td><td>2 of 39</td><td><strong>0.878</strong></td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"grc\">υἱοθεσία</span> <em>huiothesía</em> <span class=\"off\">G5206</span></td><td>3 of 27</td><td>0.915</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Greek adoption <span lang=\"grc\">υἱοθεσία</span> family</strong></td><td>3 of 27</td><td><strong>0.915</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><em>DP (Gries' deviation of proportions): how far the word's spread over the books is from what the book sizes predict.</em></p>\n<h3 id=\"8-significance-意義\">8. Significance <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">意義</span></h3>\n<p><strong>Statistical (log-likelihood G², chi-square with 1 df):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>sons-of-God Aramaic <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בַּר</span>:</strong> sections over-represented: none at p &lt; 0.001; books: Ezra (G² 24), Dan (G² 21).</li>\n<li><strong>Hebrew Nephilim <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">נְפִיל</span>:</strong> sections over-represented: none at p &lt; 0.001; books: none at p &lt; 0.001.</li>\n<li><strong>Greek adoption <span lang=\"grc\">υἱοθεσία</span>:</strong> sections over-represented: Paul's letters <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">保羅書信</span> (G² 14, p &lt; 0.001); books: Rom (G² 11).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>In context (a judgment — the rubric of the template: F first mention · P programmatic or climactic · C covenant formula or God describing himself · Q quoted in the NT or quoting the OT · A carries the argument):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Passage</th><th>F</th><th>P</th><th>C</th><th>Q</th><th>A</th><th>Rating</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.6.2\">Gen 6:2</span></td><td>F</td><td>P</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>★★</td><td>first occurrence in canonical order, and the disputed one: \"the sons of God saw the daughters of men\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.6.2\">Gen 6:2</span>, ASV)</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.6.4\">Gen 6:4</span></td><td></td><td>P</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>★</td><td>beside the Nephilim: \"when the sons of God came in unto the daughters of men\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.6.4\">Gen 6:4</span>, ASV)</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.1.6\">Job 1:6</span></td><td></td><td>P</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>★</td><td>the heavenly court, with the adversary among them: \"the sons of God came to present themselves before Jehovah\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.1.6\">Job 1:6</span>, ASV)</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.2.1\">Job 2:1</span></td><td></td><td>P</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>★</td><td>the same scene again: \"the sons of God came to present themselves before Jehovah\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.2.1\">Job 2:1</span>, ASV)</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.38.7\">Job 38:7</span></td><td></td><td>P</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>★</td><td>the one occurrence without the article, at the founding of the earth: \"all the sons of God shouted for joy?\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.38.7\">Job 38:7</span>, ASV)</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.29.1\">Ps 29:1</span></td><td>F</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>★</td><td>first occurrence of <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בְּנֵי אֵלִים</span> in canonical order: \"O ye sons of the mighty\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.29.1\">Ps 29:1</span>, ASV)</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.89.6\">Ps 89:6</span></td><td></td><td>P</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>★</td><td>beside the council of the holy ones: \"Who among the sons of the mighty is like unto Jehovah\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.89.6\">Ps 89:6</span>, ASV)</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.3.25\">Dan 3:25</span></td><td>F</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>★</td><td>the Aramaic, in a Babylonian king's mouth: \"like a son of the gods.\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.3.25\">Dan 3:25</span>, ASV)</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.82.6\">Ps 82:6</span></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>Q</td><td></td><td>★</td><td>a near relative, and Jesus quotes it (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"John.10.34\">John 10:34</span>): \"all of you sons of the Most High.\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.82.6\">Ps 82:6</span>, ASV)</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Hos.1.10\">Hos 1:10</span></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>Q</td><td></td><td>★</td><td>a near relative, of Israel, quoted at <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.9.26\">Rom 9:26</span>: \"Ye are the sons of the living God.\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Hos.1.10\">Hos 1:10</span>, ASV)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><em>Data behind \"A\" — where the words are densest (ten-verse stretches with three or more uses):</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>sons-of-God Aramaic <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בַּר</span>: <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ezra.5.1-2\">Ezra 5:1–2</span> (3).</li>\n<li>Hebrew Nephilim <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">נְפִיל</span>: no dense stretch.</li>\n<li>Greek adoption <span lang=\"grc\">υἱοθεσία</span>: no dense stretch.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"4-和合本-renderings-和合本的譯法-every-rendering-every-verse\">4. <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本</span> renderings <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本的譯法</span> (every rendering, every verse) <a class=\"up\" href=\"#1-at-a-glance-%E4%B8%80%E8%A6%BD\" title=\"回到摘要 back to the summary\">↑</a></h2>\n<p><em>From the word-by-word alignments (OT: OpenHebrewBible CUV-to-BHS; NT: OpenGNT CUVtc). Pronouns and particles at the edges are set aside for grouping (<span lang=\"zh-Hant\">他的慈愛</span> is listed under <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">慈愛</span>, and the raw forms are kept). A use the alignment does not tie to a Chinese word is listed as \"not tied\".</em></p>\n<h4 id=\"בר-bar-h1247--8-uses-2-renderings\"><big><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בַּר</span></big> <em>bar</em> <span class=\"off\">H1247</span> — 8 uses, 2 renderings</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本</span></th><th>Count</th><th>Where (by section)</th><th>Forms in the text</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">兒子</span></strong></td><td>3</td><td><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">歷史書 2, 大先知書</span> 1</td><td><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">的兒子 2, 是他的兒子</span> 1</td></tr><tr><td><strong><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">孫子</span></strong></td><td>2</td><td><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">歷史書</span> 2</td><td><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">的孫子</span> 2</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<details class=\"callout c-list\"><summary class=\"ctitle\">Every verse, by rendering (H1247)</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p><strong><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">兒子</span></strong> (3): <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ezra.5.2\">Ezra 5:2</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ezra.5.2\">5:2</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.5.22\">Dan 5:22</span><br><strong><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">孫子</span></strong> (2): <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ezra.5.1\">Ezra 5:1</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ezra.6.14\">6:14</span><br><strong>Not tied to a Chinese word by the alignment</strong> (3): <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.3.25\">Dan 3:25</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.5.31\">5:31</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.7.13\">7:13</span></p></div></details>\n<h4 id=\"נפיל-nᵉphîyl-h5303--3-uses-1-renderings\"><big><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">נְפִיל</span></big> <em>nᵉphîyl</em> <span class=\"off\">H5303</span> — 3 uses, 1 renderings</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本</span></th><th>Count</th><th>Where (by section)</th><th>Forms in the text</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">偉人</span></strong></td><td>3</td><td><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">律法書</span> 3</td><td><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">偉人 2, 他們是偉人</span> 1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<details class=\"callout c-list\"><summary class=\"ctitle\">Every verse, by rendering (H5303)</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p><strong><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">偉人</span></strong> (3): <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.6.4\">Gen 6:4</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.13.33\">Num 13:33</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.13.33\">13:33</span></p></div></details>\n<h4 id=\"υἱοθεσία-huiothesía-g5206--5-uses-2-renderings\"><big><span lang=\"grc\">υἱοθεσία</span></big> <em>huiothesía</em> <span class=\"off\">G5206</span> — 5 uses, 2 renderings</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本</span></th><th>Count</th><th>Where (by section)</th><th>Forms in the text</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">兒子的名分</span></strong></td><td>4</td><td><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">保羅書信</span> 4</td><td><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">兒子的名分 3, 得着兒子的名分</span> 1</td></tr><tr><td><strong><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">兒子</span></strong></td><td>1</td><td><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">保羅書信</span> 1</td><td><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">兒子的</span> 1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<details class=\"callout c-list\"><summary class=\"ctitle\">Every verse, by rendering (G5206)</summary><div class=\"cbody\"><p><strong><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">兒子的名分</span></strong> (4): <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.8.23\">Rom 8:23</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.9.4\">9:4</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gal.4.5\">Gal 4:5</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Eph.1.5\">Eph 1:5</span><br><strong><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">兒子</span></strong> (1): <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.8.15\">Rom 8:15</span></p></div></details>\n<p><strong>What the variety shows:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The phrase in the <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本</span>, counted by string. <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">神的眾子</span> stands in six verses: <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.1.6\">Job 1:6</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.2.1\">2:1</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.38.7\">38:7</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.29.1\">Ps 29:1</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.89.6\">89:6</span>, and <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.8.19\">Rom 8:19</span>. <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">神的兒子們</span> stands in two: <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.6.2\">Gen 6:2</span> and 6:4. The word <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">眾</span> makes the plural plain in Chinese, and the use of <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">神的眾子</span> in Job and in <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.8.19\">Rom 8:19</span> joins two different things. The Hebrew of Job says it of heavenly beings, and the Greek of <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.8.19\">Rom 8:19</span>, <em><span lang=\"grc\">τῶν υἱῶν τοῦ Θεοῦ</span></em> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.8.19\">Rom 8:19</span>, OpenGNT), of believers.</li>\n<li>Where the <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本</span> differs from the English. At <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.29.1\">Ps 29:1</span> the <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本</span> reads <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">「神的眾子啊」（</span><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.29.1\">Ps 29:1</span><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">，和合本）</span>. The ASV has \"O ye sons of the mighty,\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.29.1\">Ps 29:1</span>, ASV), and the KJV has \"O ye mighty\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.29.1\">Ps 29:1</span>, KJV), a reading that leaves out the sons. At <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.89.6\">Ps 89:6</span> the <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本</span> has <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">「神的眾子中」（</span><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.89.6\">Ps 89:6</span><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">，和合本）</span> for the ASV's \"the sons of the mighty\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.89.6\">Ps 89:6</span>, ASV). So the <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本</span> reads <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">אֵלִים</span> as God here, and ISBE records that the Revised Version's margin offers sons of God or sons of the gods at both verses.</li>\n<li>The other strings. <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">神子</span> stands once, at Dan 3:25. <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">至高者的兒子</span> stands in three verses, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.82.6\">Ps 82:6</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.1.32\">Luke 1:32</span> and 6:35. <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">永生神的兒子</span> stands in three, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Hos.1.10\">Hos 1:10</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Matt.16.16\">Matt 16:16</span> and <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.9.26\">Rom 9:26</span>. The bare string <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">神的兒子</span> stands in 49 verses, a count that includes the two <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">神的兒子們</span> verses and the three <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">永生神的兒子</span> verses. Believers and the Son are thus in the same words. <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.8.14\">Rom 8:14</span> has <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">「都是神的兒子。」（</span><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.8.14\">Rom 8:14</span><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">，和合本）</span>, and <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"John.10.36\">John 10:36</span> has the Son of himself: <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">「他自稱是神的兒子」（</span><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"John.10.36\">John 10:36</span><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">，和合本）</span>.</li>\n<li>Nephilim. <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">「那時候有偉人在地上」（</span><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.6.4\">Gen 6:4</span><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">，和合本）</span> uses <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">偉人</span> (great men), where the ASV keeps the Hebrew name, \"The Nephilim were in the earth\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.6.4\">Gen 6:4</span>, ASV), and the KJV has \"There were giants in the earth\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.6.4\">Gen 6:4</span>, KJV). So the three versions give three judgments.</li>\n<li><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.82.1\">Ps 82:1</span>. For the Hebrew <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בַּ עֲדַת־ אֵ֑ל</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.82.1\">Ps 82:1</span>, BHSA) the <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本</span> has <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">「神站在有權力者的會中」（</span><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.82.1\">Ps 82:1</span><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">，和合本）</span>, where the ASV has \"God standeth in the congregation of God;\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.82.1\">Ps 82:1</span>, ASV). That decision is treated in <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#i3adf5d2ccf\">S17</a>, sections 4 and 10.</li>\n<li>ASV counts by string. In the ASV, sons of God stands in ten verses (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.6.2\">Gen 6:2</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.6.4\">4</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.1.6\">Job 1:6</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.2.1\">2:1</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.38.7\">38:7</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Matt.5.9\">Matt 5:9</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.20.36\">Luke 20:36</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.8.14\">Rom 8:14</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.8.19\">19</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gal.3.26\">Gal 3:26</span>), sons of the mighty in two, and children of God in ten. The ASV's \"the son of God.\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.3.38\">Luke 3:38</span>, ASV) of Adam adds the word son that the Greek leaves implied (section 8).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"5-shades-of-meaning-意義層次\">5. Shades of meaning <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">意義層次</span> <a class=\"up\" href=\"#1-at-a-glance-%E4%B8%80%E8%A6%BD\" title=\"回到摘要 back to the summary\">↑</a></h2>\n<p><em>UBS Dictionary of Biblical Hebrew (SDBH 0.9.2) and UBS Greek NT Dictionary 1.1: each sense with its domain and its verses (Hebrew verse numbers mapped to the English ones).</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בַּר</span> <em>bar</em> <span class=\"off\">H1247</span>: no UBS entry<br><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">נְפִיל</span> <em>nᵉphîyl</em> <span class=\"off\">H5303</span></strong></li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Sense</th><th>Domain</th><th>Definition</th><th>Verses</th><th>For example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Nephilim</td><td>Names of Groups</td><td>literally: \"the fallen ones\"; exact meaning unsure; possibly: = a race of giants, regarded as descendants of divine beings and humans</td><td>2</td><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.6.4\">Gen 6:4</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.13.33\">Num 13:33</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong><span lang=\"grc\">υἱοθεσία</span> <em>huiothesía</em> <span class=\"off\">G5206</span></strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Sense</th><th>Domain</th><th>Definition</th><th>Verses</th><th>For example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>to adopt, adoption</td><td>Help, Care For / Adopt</td><td>to formally and legally declare that someone who is not one’s own child is henceforth to be treated and cared for as one’s own child, including complete rights …</td><td>5</td><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.8.15\">Rom 8:15</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.8.23\">Rom 8:23</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.9.4\">Rom 9:4</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"6-the-lxx-bridge-七十士譯本的橋樑\">6. The LXX bridge <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">七十士譯本的橋樑</span> <a class=\"up\" href=\"#1-at-a-glance-%E4%B8%80%E8%A6%BD\" title=\"回到摘要 back to the summary\">↑</a></h2>\n<p><em>Verse by verse: each Hebrew verse beside its Septuagint verse. <strong>Matched</strong> = verses where the Hebrew and the Greek word are paired one to one, the strongest pair first, so a Greek word already explained by another Hebrew word in the verse is not counted again: the probable rendering, to read in the verses. <strong>Together</strong> = verses where both merely stand. <strong>× chance</strong> = together ÷ what the two verse counts predict. A row with Together but Matched 0 is a companion in the verse, not a rendering.</em></p>\n<p><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בַּר</span> <em>bar</em> <span class=\"off\">H1247</span> → Greek</strong> (of 7 verses with a Septuagint verse)</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Greek in the LXX</th><th>Gloss</th><th>Matched</th><th>Together</th><th>× chance</th><th>For example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span lang=\"grc\">υἱός</span> <em>huiós</em> <span class=\"off\">G5207</span></td><td>son</td><td>3</td><td>5</td><td>4.9</td><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ezra.5.2\">Ezra 5:2</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.7.13\">Dan 7:13</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.3.25\">Dan 3:25</span></td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"grc\">προφητεία</span> <em>prophēteía</em> <span class=\"off\">G4394</span></td><td>prophecy</td><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>948.9</td><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ezra.6.14\">Ezra 6:14</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ezra.5.1\">Ezra 5:1</span></td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"grc\">θεός</span> <em>theós</em> <span class=\"off\">G2316</span></td><td>God</td><td>1</td><td>5</td><td>6.0</td><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.5.22\">Dan 5:22</span></td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"grc\">εἰμί</span> <em>eimí</em> <span class=\"off\">G1510</span></td><td>to be</td><td>1</td><td>3</td><td>2.2</td><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.5.31\">Dan 5:31</span></td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"grc\">Ζαχαρίας</span> <em>Zacharías</em> <span class=\"off\">G2197</span></td><td>Zechariah</td><td>0</td><td>2</td><td>154.5</td><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ezra.6.14\">Ezra 6:14</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ezra.5.1\">Ezra 5:1</span></td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"grc\">Ἰουδαῖος</span> <em>Ioudaîos</em> <span class=\"off\">G2453</span></td><td>Jewish</td><td>0</td><td>2</td><td>96.3</td><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ezra.6.14\">Ezra 6:14</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ezra.5.1\">Ezra 5:1</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">נְפִיל</span> <em>nᵉphîyl</em> <span class=\"off\">H5303</span> → Greek</strong> (of 2 verses with a Septuagint verse)</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table><thead><tr><th>Greek in the LXX</th><th>Gloss</th><th>Matched</th><th>Together</th><th>× chance</th><th>For example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span lang=\"grc\">γίγας</span> <span class=\"off\">G6736</span></td><td>a giant</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>750.0</td><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.13.33\">Num 13:33</span></td></tr><tr><td><span lang=\"grc\">εἰμί</span> <em>eimí</em> <span class=\"off\">G1510</span></td><td>to be</td><td>0</td><td>2</td><td>5.2</td><td><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.13.33\">Num 13:33</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.6.4\">Gen 6:4</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong><span lang=\"grc\">υἱοθεσία</span> <em>huiothesía</em> <span class=\"off\">G5206</span>:</strong> not in the LXX notes (0 verses with a Hebrew verse beside it), so there is no bridge to read.</p>\n<p><strong>OT verses with these Hebrew words that the NT quotes</strong> (chart <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">舊約在新約</span> The Old Testament in the New, verse by verse: a list, or OpenBible where the tagged words have no list address, names the link, the NT words are tagged as an OT quotation (Levinsohn), and the two verses share words): <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.7.13\">Dan 7:13</span> → <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Matt.24.30\">Matt 24:30</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Matt.26.64\">Matt 26:64</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Mark.13.26\">Mark 13:26</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Mark.14.62\">Mark 14:62</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.21.27\">Luke 21:27</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>Also named as quoted or referenced</strong> (\"Old in New\", Nave: the lists do not sort allusion from quotation; kept only where these two verses share words or OpenBible readers link exactly these two verses): <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.7.13\">Dan 7:13</span> → <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rev.1.7\">Rev 1:7</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rev.14.14\">Rev 14:14</span>.</p>\n<p><em>Left out, too loose at verse level: 3 NT verses a list names only as part of a longer passage, without shared wording or reader links strong enough at that verse (all stand in the chart).</em></p>\n<p><em>Also 1 verses that stand inside a longer OT passage the NT refers to (for example Stephen's speech in Acts 7 or the warnings in Jude): <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.3.25\">Dan 3:25</span>.</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Two Greek texts are used here, and they do not agree. The LXX used here is the Apostolic Bible Polyglot text (the Sixtine of 1587 by way of Bos 1709, not Rahlfs-Hanhart, with Theodotion for Daniel). Swete's Septuagint is the First1KGreek transcription.</li>\n<li><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.6.2\">Gen 6:2</span> and 6:4. The Greek used here has <em><span lang=\"grc\">οι υιοί του θεού</span></em> (LXX <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.6.2\">Gen 6:2</span>, ABP) and <em><span lang=\"grc\">οι υιοί του θεού</span></em> (LXX <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.6.4\">Gen 6:4</span>, ABP). Swete's text has <span lang=\"grc\">οἱ ἄγγελοι τοῦ</span> at <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.6.2\">Gen 6:2</span> (the word after that is lost in the transcription) and <span lang=\"grc\">οἱ υἱοὶ τοῦ θεοῦ</span> at <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.6.4\">Gen 6:4</span>. Alford reports Bleek's view that in the Septuagint <span lang=\"grc\">ἄγγελοι</span> stands at <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.1.6\">Job 1:6</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.2.1\">2:1</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.38.7\">38:7</span> and <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.3.25\">Dan 3:25</span> and that at <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.6.2\">Gen 6:2</span> it stands only in the Alexandrian manuscript and in Philo ('and there in the alex. Ms. and Philo'). Charles, in his note on 1 Enoch 6, gives <span lang=\"grc\">ἄγγελοι</span> as the original Septuagint rendering. So the angel reading stands in Greek manuscripts as well as in interpretation.</li>\n<li><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.1.6\">Job 1:6</span> and 2:1. The Greek used here has <em><span lang=\"grc\">οι υιοί του θεού</span></em> (LXX <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.1.6\">Job 1:6</span>, ABP) and <em><span lang=\"grc\">οι υιοί του θεού</span></em> (LXX <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.2.1\">Job 2:1</span>, ABP). Swete's text has <span lang=\"grc\">οἱ ἄγγελοι τοῦ θεοῦ</span> in both, and ISBE prints that reading for both. So a reader of the text used here would not see what the printed Septuagint of ISBE's day shows, and this needs a critical edition.</li>\n<li><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.38.7\">Job 38:7</span>. Both Greek texts have <em><span lang=\"grc\">πάντες άγγελοί μου</span></em> (LXX <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.38.7\">Job 38:7</span>, ABP) (all my angels), a rendering that takes the phrase as angels and adds the pronoun. At this verse the Greek of Job interprets the phrase as angels in both texts.</li>\n<li><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.29.1\">Ps 29:1</span> (LXX 28:1) and <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.89.6\">Ps 89:6</span> (LXX 88:7). Both texts have the sons wording: <em><span lang=\"grc\">υιοί θεού</span></em> (LXX <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.29.1\">Ps 29:1</span>, ABP) and <em><span lang=\"grc\">εν υιοίς θεού</span></em> (LXX <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.89.6\">Ps 89:6</span>, ABP). <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.29.1\">Ps 29:1</span> then adds <em><span lang=\"grc\">υιούς κριών</span></em> (LXX <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.29.1\">Ps 29:1</span>, ABP), sons of rams, a line with no separate counterpart in the Hebrew of the verse. The word for ram, <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">אַיִל</span> (H352), is close in form to <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">אֵלִים</span>, which may lie behind it (an inference, not drawn from a source opened here). <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.82.6\">Ps 82:6</span> (LXX 81:6) reads <em><span lang=\"grc\">υιοί υψίστου</span></em> (LXX <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.82.6\">Ps 82:6</span>, ABP) in both.</li>\n<li><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.3.25\">Dan 3:25</span> in Theodotion (LXX 3:92 in Swete). Both have <em><span lang=\"grc\">ομοία υιώ θεού</span></em> (LXX <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.3.25\">Dan 3:25</span>, ABP), singular without the article. <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Hos.1.10\">Hos 1:10</span> has <em><span lang=\"grc\">υιοί θεού ζώντος</span></em> (LXX <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Hos.1.10\">Hos 1:10</span>, ABP), which <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.9.26\">Rom 9:26</span> quotes.</li>\n<li><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Deut.32.43\">Deut 32:43</span> in the Greek used here has both phrases in the longer text: <em><span lang=\"grc\">και προσκυνησάτωσαν αυτώ πάντες άγγελοι θεού</span></em> (LXX <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Deut.32.43\">Deut 32:43</span>, ABP) and <em><span lang=\"grc\">και ενισχυσάτωσαν αυτώ πάντες υιοί θεού</span></em> (LXX <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Deut.32.43\">Deut 32:43</span>, ABP). Swete's transcription has only <span lang=\"grc\">υἱοὶ</span> in the first of those lines, with a word lost after it. The Hebrew of BHSA has neither. S24, section 6, treats the use of this line at <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Heb.1.6\">Heb 1:6</span> and is not repeated here.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"7-companions-and-cross-references-同伴字與互參\">7. Companions and cross-references <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">同伴字與互參</span> <a class=\"up\" href=\"#1-at-a-glance-%E4%B8%80%E8%A6%BD\" title=\"回到摘要 back to the summary\">↑</a></h2>\n<p><em>Words that stand with it far more often than chance. Same verse, and within two verses either side (same book). Ranked by G²; PMI = log₂(observed ÷ expected): 1 = twice as often as chance, 3 = eight times.</em></p>\n<p><strong>sons-of-God Aramaic <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בַּר</span>, same verse:</strong> <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">אֱלָהּ</span> god (4; PMI 7.41, G² 36).</p>\n<p><strong>sons-of-God Aramaic <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בַּר</span>, within two verses:</strong> <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">מַלְכוּ</span> kingdom (6; PMI 7.37, G² 58), <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">אָתָה</span> come (5; PMI 7.95, G² 50), <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">קוּם</span> stand (5; PMI 7.17, G² 45), <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שְׁנָה</span> year (4; PMI 8.69, G² 44), <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">פֶּחָה</span> governor (4; PMI 8.37, G² 42), <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">דָּֽרְיָוֵשׁ</span> Darius (4; PMI 8.14, G² 40), <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">אֱלָהּ</span> god (5; PMI 6.5, G² 40), <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">נְבִיא</span> prophet (3; PMI 9.95, G² 38), <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">מֶלֶךְ</span> king (5; PMI 6.13, G² 38), <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בַּיִת</span> house (4; PMI 7.09, G² 34).</p>\n\n<p><strong>Hebrew Nephilim <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">נְפִיל</span>, within two verses:</strong> .</p>\n\n<p><strong>Greek adoption <span lang=\"grc\">υἱοθεσία</span>, within two verses:</strong> <span lang=\"grc\">πατήρ</span> father (4; PMI 2.28, G² 10), <span lang=\"grc\">δόξα</span> glory (3; PMI 2.91, G² 9), <span lang=\"grc\">θεός</span> God (5; PMI 1.12, G² 8), <span lang=\"grc\">πνεῦμα</span> spirit/breath (3; PMI 1.81, G² 5), <span lang=\"grc\">Χριστός</span> Christ (3; PMI 1.53, G² 4).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Satan among them. <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">וַ יָּבֹ֥וא גַֽם־ הַ שָּׂטָ֖ן בְּ תֹוכָֽם׃</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.1.6\">Job 1:6</span>, BHSA), \"that Satan also came among them.\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.1.6\">Job 1:6</span>, ASV) The word among says he comes in their midst, and it leaves open whether he counts as one of them.</li>\n<li>The court in other words. Ps 89 sets the phrase beside \"the assembly of the holy ones.\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.89.5\">Ps 89:5</span>, ASV) and \"the council of the holy ones,\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.89.7\">Ps 89:7</span>, ASV). <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.82.1\">Ps 82:1</span> has \"the congregation of God;\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.82.1\">Ps 82:1</span>, ASV), and <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"1Kgs.22.19\">1 Kgs 22:19</span> has \"all the host of heaven standing by him\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"1Kgs.22.19\">1 Kgs 22:19</span>, ASV). The treatment of the council in <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#i77c1eaff31\">S28</a>, section 10, stands behind these lines.</li>\n<li>Parallel with the morning stars. In <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.38.7\">Job 38:7</span> the two lines of the verse stand side by side: \"the morning stars sang together\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.38.7\">Job 38:7</span>, ASV) and \"all the sons of God shouted for joy?\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.38.7\">Job 38:7</span>, ASV) Whether that parallel makes the sons of God stars or only sets the two together is a matter of reading the poetry.</li>\n<li>Israel and the king. \"Ye are the children of Jehovah your God:\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Deut.14.1\">Deut 14:1</span>, ASV) (<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בָּנִ֣ים אַתֶּ֔ם לַֽ יהוָ֖ה אֱלֹהֵיכֶ֑ם</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Deut.14.1\">Deut 14:1</span>, BHSA)); \"I will be his father, and he shall be my son:\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"2Sam.7.14\">2 Sam 7:14</span>, ASV); \"Thou art my son;\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.2.7\">Ps 2:7</span>, ASV). Keil cites <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.73.15\">Ps 73:15</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Deut.32.5\">Deut 32:5</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Hos.1.10\">Hos 1:10</span> and <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.80.17\">Ps 80:17</span> in order to show that the term is not confined to angels. These are the passages behind the second classic reading of Gen 6 (section 10), and <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Heb.1.5\">Heb 1:5</span> quotes <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.2.7\">Ps 2:7</span> and <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"2Sam.7.14\">2 Sam 7:14</span> of the Son.</li>\n<li>Verse cross-references: \"Old in New\" is used in section 6.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"8-in-the-new-testament-新約的用法\">8. In the New Testament <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">新約的用法</span> <a class=\"up\" href=\"#1-at-a-glance-%E4%B8%80%E8%A6%BD\" title=\"回到摘要 back to the summary\">↑</a></h2>\n<p>By section (from 3.3): Gospels 0, Acts 0, Paul's letters 5, Hebrews and the General letters 0, Revelation 0.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Counts (OpenGNT).</strong> <span lang=\"grc\">υἱός</span> stands 377 times and <span lang=\"grc\">τέκνον</span> 99 times. The plural <span lang=\"grc\">υἱοὶ Θεοῦ</span>, of people, stands in six verses: <em><span lang=\"grc\">αὐτοὶ υἱοὶ Θεοῦ κληθήσονται</span></em> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Matt.5.9\">Matt 5:9</span>, OpenGNT), <em><span lang=\"grc\">καὶ υἱοί εἰσιν Θεοῦ</span></em> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.20.36\">Luke 20:36</span>, OpenGNT), <em><span lang=\"grc\">οὗτοι υἱοί Θεοῦ εἰσιν</span></em> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.8.14\">Rom 8:14</span>, OpenGNT), <em><span lang=\"grc\">τῶν υἱῶν τοῦ Θεοῦ</span></em> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.8.19\">Rom 8:19</span>, OpenGNT), <em><span lang=\"grc\">Υἱοὶ Θεοῦ ζῶντος</span></em> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.9.26\">Rom 9:26</span>, OpenGNT) (from Hosea) and <em><span lang=\"grc\">Πάντες γὰρ υἱοὶ Θεοῦ ἐστε</span></em> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gal.3.26\">Gal 3:26</span>, OpenGNT). The phrase <em><span lang=\"grc\">υἱοὶ Ὑψίστου</span></em> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.6.35\">Luke 6:35</span>, OpenGNT) is a seventh, close by. <span lang=\"grc\">τέκνα Θεοῦ</span> stands in ten verses: <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"John.1.12\">John 1:12</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"John.11.52\">11:52</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.8.16\">Rom 8:16</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.8.21\">21</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.9.8\">9:8</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Phil.2.15\">Phil 2:15</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"1John.3.1\">1 John 3:1</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"1John.3.2\">2</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"1John.3.10\">10</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"1John.5.2\">5:2</span>. The singular <span lang=\"grc\">υἱός</span> with the genitive <span lang=\"grc\">Θεοῦ</span>, at most one word between them, stands in 40 verses, all of Jesus.</li>\n<li><strong>The difference in kind.</strong> In the Old Testament verses of this study the phrase is said of heavenly beings (Job; Ps 29; 89), of Israel (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Hos.1.10\">Hos 1:10</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Exod.4.22\">Exod 4:22</span> in the singular), and of those addressed in <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.82.6\">Ps 82:6</span>. In the New Testament, believers are sons by a gift: \"to them gave he the right to become children of God\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"John.1.12\">John 1:12</span>, ASV), \"For as many as are led by the Spirit of God, these are sons of God.\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.8.14\">Rom 8:14</span>, ASV), and \"For ye are all sons of God, through faith, in Christ Jesus.\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gal.3.26\">Gal 3:26</span>, ASV) ISBE: 'Men are not by nature the sons of God', and 'Men become sons of God in the regenerative and adoptive sense'. <span lang=\"grc\">υἱοθεσία</span> stands for the legal act. Abbott-Smith lists <span lang=\"grc\">τέκνα Θεοῦ</span> under the metaphorical use of <span lang=\"grc\">τέκνον</span>, 'with reference to the Fatherhood of God'.</li>\n<li><strong>John and Paul.</strong> ISBE says John uses <span lang=\"grc\">τέκνον</span> and Paul <span lang=\"grc\">υἱός</span>, with the legal side in view. The counts agree only in part. John's Gospel and first letter use <span lang=\"grc\">τέκνα Θεοῦ</span> of believers. Paul uses <span lang=\"grc\">υἱοί</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.8.14\">Rom 8:14</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.8.19\">19</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gal.3.26\">Gal 3:26</span>) and he also uses <span lang=\"grc\">τέκνα</span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.8.16\">Rom 8:16</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.8.21\">21</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.9.8\">9:8</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Phil.2.15\">Phil 2:15</span>). So in Rom 8 the two words stand side by side.</li>\n<li><strong>Still to come.</strong> \"For the earnest expectation of the creation waiteth for the revealing of the sons of God.\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.8.19\">Rom 8:19</span>, ASV) and \"waiting for our adoption, to wit, the redemption of our body.\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.8.23\">Rom 8:23</span>, ASV) The present word is at <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"1John.3.2\">1 John 3:2</span>, \"now are we children of God, and it is not yet made manifest what we shall be.\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"1John.3.2\">1 John 3:2</span>, ASV)</li>\n<li><strong>Equal to the angels, and sons.</strong> \"for they are equal unto the angels; and are sons of God, being sons of the resurrection.\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.20.36\">Luke 20:36</span>, ASV) That verse puts the resurrected next to the angels without calling the angels sons. It is the only one of the six verses with the angels in it. <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Matt.22.30\">Matt 22:30</span> and <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Mark.12.25\">Mark 12:25</span> say they are \"as angels in heaven.\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Matt.22.30\">Matt 22:30</span>, ASV)</li>\n<li><strong>Jesus on Ps 82.</strong> \"If he called them gods, unto whom the word of God came (and the scripture cannot be broken),\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"John.10.35\">John 10:35</span>, ASV), and in the next verse \"I am the Son of God?\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"John.10.36\">John 10:36</span>, ASV) Jesus' argument moves from the lesser title to the greater one.</li>\n<li><strong>Adam.</strong> The ASV has \"the son of Adam, the son of God.\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.3.38\">Luke 3:38</span>, ASV) The Greek has only the article at each step of Luke's list, <em><span lang=\"grc\">τοῦ Ἀδὰμ τοῦ Θεοῦ</span></em> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Luke.3.38\">Luke 3:38</span>, OpenGNT), and <span lang=\"grc\">υἱός</span> stands at the head of the list. Luke's line of descent ends in God, without a word about what kind of son Adam is.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"9-culture-and-background-文化背景\">9. Culture and background <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">文化背景</span> <a class=\"up\" href=\"#1-at-a-glance-%E4%B8%80%E8%A6%BD\" title=\"回到摘要 back to the summary\">↑</a></h2>\n<ul>\n<li>1 Enoch. Chapter 6 retells Genesis as an angel story: 'And the angels, the children of the heaven, saw and lusted after them'. It names their leader Semjaza and says they came down 'on the summit of Mount Hermon' (R. H. Charles's translation, 1913). Charles comments: 'This refers not to alliances between Sethites and Cainites', and he reports Bousset's suggestion of a Persian myth behind the story. Jubilees 5:1 has 'the angels of God saw them on a certain year of this jubilee'.</li>\n<li>The Targums. In J. W. Etheridge's 1862 translation, Onkelos at <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.6.2\">Gen 6:2</span> has 'the sons of the mighty', with the note 'Bnei rabrebaia' and, for the Samaritan Version, 'sons of the rulers'. Pseudo-Jonathan has 'the sons of the great', and at verse 4 it has 'Schamchazai and Uzziel, who fell from heaven', the names found in the Enoch tradition. So one Targum carries both the rulers and the fallen angels. The dating of the Targums is debated.</li>\n<li>At Qumran (ETCBC edition of Abegg, Bowley and Cook; several readings stand in the editors' restorations). The phrases stand nine times: 4Q37 12:14 at <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Deut.32.8\">Deut 32:8</span>, with <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בני אלוהים</span>; <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בני אלים</span> at 1QHa 23:23, 4Q491 f24:4 and 5/6Hev1b f13i:4 (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.29.1\">Ps 29:1</span>, restored); <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בני אל</span> at 1QHa 24:33 and 11Q13 ii:14, and in two copies of <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Hos.1.10\">Hos 1:10</span> (4Q79 and 4Q82, restored); and <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בני עליון</span> at Mas1e 2:10 (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.82.6\">Ps 82:6</span>). <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Deut.32.43\">Deut 32:43</span> at 4Q44 f5ii:7 has <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">השתחוו לו כל אלהים</span>, a line the BHSA text does not have.</li>\n<li>Ugarit and the ancient Near East: the Ugaritic texts of the second millennium BC have the phrase the sons of El for the gods of El's assembly. Scholars such as M. S. Smith have read Israel's phrases against that background. Whether Israel's use preserves that older meaning, or uses it to deny it, is the debate of section 10.</li>\n<li>Later Jewish and Christian writers, as listed in Charles's note on 1 Enoch 6. Charles names as dependent on the Enoch account, or harmonizing with it, Jubilees, the Testaments of Reuben and Naphtali, 2 Enoch, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Jude.1.6\">Jude 6</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"2Pet.2.4\">2 Pet 2:4</span>, Josephus (<em>Antiquities</em> 1.3.1), Philo (<em>On the Giants</em>), Justin Martyr, Clement of Alexandria, Tertullian, Lactantius and Commodian. He adds that Augustine, at <em>City of God</em> 15.23, opposes this view and denies the inspiration of Enoch, which Tertullian had upheld.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"10-theology-神學\">10. Theology <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">神學</span> <a class=\"up\" href=\"#1-at-a-glance-%E4%B8%80%E8%A6%BD\" title=\"回到摘要 back to the summary\">↑</a></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Who are the sons of God in <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.6.1-4\">Gen 6:1–4</span>? The debated point.</strong> Keil names three readings: 'the sons of princes, (b) angels, (c) the Sethites or godly men'. <em>Rulers.</em> This is the Targums' direction (section 9), ISBE says 'This is the view of many Jewish authorities' on the ground that <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">אֱלֹהִים</span> can mean judges (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Exod.21.6\">Exod 21:6</span>), and it objects that where man means the lower classes the contrast is with another word for man, as at <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.49.2\">Ps 49:2</span>. Smith's <em>Dictionary</em> calls the title here 'applied to the potentates of the earth', citing Cyril of Alexandria. Keil calls it the traditional view in rabbinical Judaism and says it 'may be dismissed at once as not warranted by the usages of the language, and as altogether unscriptural'. M. G. Kline's 1962 study of divine kingship is a modern form of this view. <em>The line of Seth.</em> Keil holds this: the phrase can be applied to God's people, so it 'cannot be elucidated by philological means, but must be interpreted by theology alone', and he points to 'the pious, who walked with God and called upon the name of the Lord, had been mentioned just before'. ISBE reports the view that the sons of God are 'the pious race descended from Seth', tied to <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.4.25-26\">Gen 4:25–26</span>, where Seth's line calls on Jehovah. Charles reports that Augustine combats the angel reading at <em>City of God</em> 15.23; Julius Africanus is the other classic name. <em>Angels.</em> Keil himself grants that in Job and <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.3.25\">Dan 3:25</span> they are 'unquestionably angels', and he also grants that apart from context the phrase and the contrast with the daughters of men would point that way. 1 Enoch, Jubilees and many Fathers read it so (section 9), and ISBE says of the human readings, 'Most scholars now reject this view' (1915), reading supernatural beings as in the other passages. Wenham and others read the sons of God as heavenly beings. The objection is the one Keil presses: the verb for taking wives 'is a standing expression throughout the whole of the Old Testament' for marriage, and 'Christ Himself distinctly states that the angels cannot marry'. Kurtz and Hofmann replied, and Keil answers them. The counts establish one thing only: outside Gen 6 the exact phrase stands only in Job (1:6; 2:1; 38:7), and there it belongs to the heavenly court or to the founding of the earth. Whether Gen 6 follows that usage is decided by the context and by theology, and the context is short.</li>\n<li><strong>Nephilim.</strong> Keil distinguishes the two: the verse sets them down before the marriages, and he derives the word from a verb meaning to fall upon, so that it 'signifies the invaders'; at <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Num.13.33\">Num 13:33</span> he says the word 'cannot signify giants', and he adds that 'The explanation \"fallen from heaven\" needs no refutation'. ISBE (James Orr) holds: 'In the former place the Nephilim are not necessarily to be identified with the children'. BDB glosses giants, following the Greek, and calls the etymology dubious. The text says only that they were there, \"and also after that,\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.6.4\">Gen 6:4</span>, ASV). The relation of the Nephilim to the offspring is not stated.</li>\n<li><strong><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Deut.32.8\">Deut 32:8</span>: which text? The debated point.</strong> The Masoretic Text has \"According to the number of the children of Israel.\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Deut.32.8\">Deut 32:8</span>, ASV) The Greek used here and Swete's text have the number of the angels of God. 4Q37 at Qumran has <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בני אלוהים</span> at this place; in the local edition those two words are extant and the word before them is restored. Keil says the Septuagint rendering 'is of no critical value' and that it rests on 'the later Jewish notion of guardian angels of the different nations'; he reads the Masoretic text to mean 'that Israel might receive as its inheritance a land proportioned to its numbers'. Many scholars treat sons of God as the older reading, altered in the Masoretic tradition to avoid a polytheistic sense, and the Göttingen LXX prints sons of God on the strength of a papyrus; some translations follow it. The local data shows the three readings stand. It cannot show which is original. Verse 9, \"For Jehovah's portion is his people;\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Deut.32.9\">Deut 32:9</span>, ASV), stands with either reading, and S27, section 6, gives the place of this verse in the idea of heavenly princes of the nations.</li>\n<li><strong><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.29.1\">Ps 29:1</span> and <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.89.6\">Ps 89:6</span>: sons of God or sons of gods?</strong> The ASV has sons of the mighty and the <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本 神的眾子</span>. Delitzsch says 'It is not the mighty of the earth', they are the angels, and that the plural must be rendered '\"sons of God\", not \"sons of gods.\"', calling them ones 'who form with God their Father as it were one family'. His grammatical point is that <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">אֵלִים</span> is a double plural of a construct expression (his comparison is at <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Isa.42.22\">Isa 42:22</span>). Keil also counts these among the angels. Others read the plural as a real plural of gods.</li>\n<li><strong>Who are the gods of Ps 82?</strong> Three positions stand. Delitzsch holds that they are the judges and rulers of Israel: 'they are really elohim by the grace of God'. He names Hupfeld and Bleek, who read angels, and he answers them, 'But the angels as such are nowhere called elohim in the Old Testament', with the qualification 'although they might be so called'. Many modern scholars read Ps 82 as a scene of the divine council with real gods, sentenced to die like men. The verse says \"Nevertheless ye shall die like men,\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.82.7\">Ps 82:7</span>, ASV), and that line is the difficulty for every reading: judges die already, and heavenly beings ordinarily do not. S17, section 10, carries the same debate. Jesus' use of the verse at John 10:34–36 turns on those \"unto whom the word of God came\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"John.10.35\">John 10:35</span>, ASV), a phrase of his that fits the people addressed by God's word and does not by itself settle who they are.</li>\n<li><strong>The divine-council view.</strong> <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.82.1\">Ps 82:1</span> has God standing in a gathering, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.89.7\">Ps 89:7</span> has a council, and Job has a court where beings present themselves. Scholars such as M. S. Smith and Heiser read these passages against the assembly of the gods in Ugarit, and some read Israel's own earlier belief behind them; others hold that Israel uses a shared image of a royal court to say that all such beings are the servants of the one God. The Bible's own statements are firm against a rival: \"Who among the sons of the mighty is like unto Jehovah,\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.89.6\">Ps 89:6</span>, ASV) and \"And there is no god with me:\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Deut.32.39\">Deut 32:39</span>, ASV). S28, section 10, treats the council on the side of the host.</li>\n<li><strong>Who stood in the fire?</strong> The king says the fourth is \"like a son of the gods.\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.3.25\">Dan 3:25</span>, ASV) Keil reads this as 'to a son of the gods, i.e. to one of the race of the gods' and says 'he speaks in the spirit and meaning of the Babylonian doctrine of the gods', adding 'In ver. 28 the same personage is called an angel of God', where the king says \"who hath sent his angel\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Dan.3.28\">Dan 3:28</span>, ASV). BDB has 'a divine (or angelic) being'. Reading the figure as the pre-incarnate Son is a Christian interpretation found in the KJV's capitals and in many commentators. The Aramaic has an indefinite singular and a plural noun, and the words are a Babylonian's.</li>\n<li><strong>The spirits in prison, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"1Pet.3.19-20\">1 Pet 3:19–20</span>. The debated point.</strong> \"in which also he went and preached unto the spirits in prison,\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"1Pet.3.19\">1 Pet 3:19</span>, ASV) and \"that aforetime were disobedient, when the longsuffering of God waited in the days of Noah\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"1Pet.3.20\">1 Pet 3:20</span>, ASV). Alford, following Huther, lists the positions. Some say Christ preached by Noah's mouth in Noah's time ('Augustine, Bede, Thos. Aquinas, Lyra, Hammond, Beza, Scaliger, Leighton, Horneius, Gerhard'); Alford rejects that. Alford himself holds that Christ went to the place of departed spirits, 'the disembodied spirits, which were kept shut up', and that 'they were those of men who were formerly disobedient', and he adds, 'What was the intent of that preaching, and what its effect is not here revealed; the fact merely is stated.' Bengel says this 'may be understood to have been to some a preaching of the Gospel' and to others a publishing of the law. E. G. Selwyn and others read the spirits as the fallen angels of Gen 6, to whom Christ proclaimed victory. On that reading the verse belongs to this study; on Alford's reading it does not. The text says spirits and Noah's days. It does not say angels and it does not say men.</li>\n<li><strong><span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"2Pet.2.4\">2 Pet 2:4</span> and <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Jude.1.6\">Jude 6</span>. The debated point.</strong> \"For if God spared not angels when they sinned, but cast them down to hell,\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"2Pet.2.4\">2 Pet 2:4</span>, ASV), and \"And angels that kept not their own principality, but left their proper habitation,\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Jude.1.6\">Jude 1:6</span>, ASV). The verb at <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"2Pet.2.4\">2 Pet 2:4</span> is <span lang=\"grc\">ταρταρώσας</span>: Alford says 'Tartarus is no where else mentioned in the N. T. or LXX', and Bengel that 'it does not occur elsewhere in the New Testament, nor in the Septuagint'. Alford on Jude: 'The fact alluded to is probably that which is obscurely indicated in Gen. vi. 2.' On <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Jude.1.7\">Jude 7</span>'s 'in like manner' he writes 'The manner was similar, because the angels committed fornication with another race than themselves'. Keil replies: 'Nor do 2 Pet. ii. 4 and <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Jude.1.6\">Jude 6</span> furnish any evidence of angel marriages.' Charles lists both verses among those dependent on or harmonizing with the Enoch account. Neither verse names Gen 6. Jude does cite Enoch by name at verse 14, so the likelihood that he has Enoch's account in mind is strong, though Enoch is not named at verse 6.</li>\n<li><strong>Son and sons.</strong> The one Son is not a member of a class. Alford says of <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Heb.1.5\">Heb 1:5</span>, 'The angels themselves are called' sons of God in the Old Testament, but the title is given to Christ 'in a sense in which it never was conferred upon an angel'. The plural and the singular therefore do different work, and a reader who treats them alike confuses the Old Testament phrase with the title of the Son. Nor do the sons of Rom 8 become what the Son is by nature: they are sons by adoption, which is Paul's word.</li>\n<li>Where the traditions differ (one neutral line): many patristic writers read Gen 6 of angels and later Christian writers, from Augustine onward, generally of the line of Seth; Jewish tradition moved from angels (Enoch) to judges and rulers; modern interpreters are divided, and the angelic reading has returned.</li>\n</ul>\n\n\n\n\n\n<h2 id=\"12-for-practice-應用\">11. For practice <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">應用</span> <a class=\"up\" href=\"#1-at-a-glance-%E4%B8%80%E8%A6%BD\" title=\"回到摘要 back to the summary\">↑</a></h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Teaching slide: <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בְּנֵי הָאֱלֹהִים</span> = sons of God = <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">神的眾子</span> / <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">神的兒子們</span>; eight places in the Old Testament, four with the article, two with <span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">אֵלִים</span>, one without the article, and one Aramaic. In the New Testament the plural is said of believers.</li>\n<li>Bible class: read <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.1.6\">Job 1:6</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.2.1\">2:1</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.38.7\">38:7</span>, then <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.6.1-4\">Gen 6:1–4</span>. Ask what the Job passages make likely for Genesis, and what <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Matt.22.30\">Matt 22:30</span> and the context of Gen 6 make difficult. Set out the three readings on one page, each with the best evidence for it and against it. Then read <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.8.14-23\">Rom 8:14–23</span> and ask what is different.</li>\n<li>Personal: \"Behold what manner of love the Father hath bestowed upon us, that we should be called children of God;\" (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"1John.3.1\">1 John 3:1</span>, ASV). The Old Testament phrase names beings of God's court. The same family name is given here to forgiven sinners, and the verse says what kind of love that is.</li>\n<li>For preachers: <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.6.1-4\">Gen 6:1–4</span> can be preached without settling its identity if the point stays where the passage puts it, in <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.6.5\">Gen 6:5</span>. State the readings plainly. Do not build doctrine on the Nephilim, and do not quote 1 Enoch as Scripture.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">連在一起</span> Connected</strong> — how they relate · why it matters · how we live it out:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bible truths <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">聖經真理</span>:</strong> <span class=\"off\">93 <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">天使是受造的</span></span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.38.7\">Job 38:7</span>) · <span class=\"off\">94 <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">服役的靈</span></span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.29.1\">Ps 29:1</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Heb.1.14\">Heb 1:14</span>) · <span class=\"off\">96 <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">基督超乎天使</span></span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Heb.1.5\">Heb 1:5</span>) · <span class=\"off\">55 <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">兒女的名分</span></span> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.8.15\">Rom 8:15</span>, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.8.23\">23</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gal.4.5\">Gal 4:5</span>).</li>\n<li><strong>Book studies <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">書卷研究</span>:</strong> <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#i7a592efe93\">Hebrews</a> (1:5; 2:10; 12:7) · <span class=\"off\">Romans</span> (8:14–23) · <span class=\"off\">Genesis</span> (6:1–4) · <span class=\"off\">Job</span> (1:6; 2:1; 38:7) · <span class=\"off\">Psalms</span> (29; 82; 89) · <span class=\"off\">Daniel</span> (3:25) · <span class=\"off\">Hosea</span> (1:10) · <span class=\"off\">Jude</span> (verses 6–7).</li>\n<li><strong>World Watch <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">世界觀察</span>:</strong> <span class=\"off\">the Dead Sea Scrolls</span> · 4Q37 at <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Deut.32.8\">Deut 32:8</span> and 4Q44 at <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Deut.32.43\">Deut 32:43</span> (sections 9 and 10).</li>\n<li><strong>The Lampstand 燈臺: </strong>knop 1, the Word of God · a phrase whose meaning turns on the text.</li>\n<li><strong>Word studies <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">原文研究</span>:</strong> <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#ifc420ed831\">S24 angel</a> (the whole family of the word, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Heb.1.6\">Heb 1:6</span> among them; its ninth section is the starting point on the sons of God) · <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#i3adf5d2ccf\">S17 God</a> (the Elohim of Ps 82) · <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#i9a8045aeae\">S27 Michael</a> (<span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Deut.32.8\">Deut 32:8</span> and the princes of the nations) · <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#i77c1eaff31\">S28 host</a> (the council) · <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#i207624e2ed\">S29 Gabriel</a> (the angels who stand before God) · <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#i1b5f27bacc\">S18 Messiah</a> (the Son of Ps 2).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong><span lang=\"zh-Hant\">標籤詞</span> Tag words</strong> — <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">中文</span> · English · Hebrew · Greek (the shared vocabulary; counts = words in the text; a Hebrew or Greek word opens its word study, or its lexicon entry where no study exists yet):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>#t/sons-of-god <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">神的眾子</span> · sons of God · <span class=\"off\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בֵּן</span></span> H1121 (4,946)</li>\n<li>#t/son <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">子</span> · Son · <span class=\"off\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בֵּן</span></span> H1121 (4,946) · <span class=\"off\"><span lang=\"grc\">υἱός</span></span> G5207 (377)</li>\n<li>#t/angel <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">天使</span> · angel · <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#ifc420ed831\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">מַלְאָךְ</span></a> H4397 (213) · <a class=\"wl\" href=\"#ifc420ed831\"><span lang=\"grc\">ἄγγελος</span></a> G32 (175)</li>\n<li>#t/adoption <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">兒子的名分</span> · adoption · <span class=\"off\"><span lang=\"grc\">υἱοθεσία</span></span> G5206 (5)</li>\n<li>#t/satan <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">撒但</span> · Satan · <span class=\"off\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">שָׂטָן</span></span> H7854 (27) · <span class=\"off\"><span lang=\"grc\">Σατανᾶς</span></span> G4567 (36)</li>\n<li>grammar <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">文法</span>: <span class=\"off\">construct chain <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">附屬鏈</span> (<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">בְּנֵ֣י אֵלִ֑ים</span>, Ps 29:1)</span> (<span class=\"off\">page 4 <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">圖</span></span>) · <span class=\"off\">imperfect <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">未完成式</span> (<span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"he\">יָבֹ֜אוּ</span>, Gen 6:4)</span> (<span class=\"off\">page 2 <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">圖</span></span>)</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"13-evaluation-評估\">12. Evaluation <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">評估</span> <a class=\"up\" href=\"#1-at-a-glance-%E4%B8%80%E8%A6%BD\" title=\"回到摘要 back to the summary\">↑</a></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Why it matters:</strong> the phrase is short and the questions are large. It bears on angels, on salvation by adoption and on the Son. Rom 8 and Gen 6 use the same words and do not mean the same thing.</li>\n<li><strong>Where it is strongest:</strong> <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.1.6\">Job 1:6</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.2.1\">2:1</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.38.7\">38:7</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.29.1\">Ps 29:1</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Ps.89.6\">89:6</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Rom.8.14-23\">Rom 8:14–23</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gal.3.26\">Gal 3:26</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Heb.1.5\">Heb 1:5</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Heb.2.10\">2:10</span>.</li>\n<li><strong>For whom:</strong> teachers facing <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.6.1-4\">Gen 6:1–4</span>, readers of the Psalms who meet the word gods, and believers who need to know what the name sons of God means for them.</li>\n<li><strong>Limits:</strong> eight places, four of them in two short scenes. The word usage favours heavenly beings where the exact phrase occurs. It does not settle Gen 6, and the questions about <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Deut.32.8\">Deut 32:8</span>, Ps 82, <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"1Pet.3.19\">1 Pet 3:19</span> and <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Jude.1.6\">Jude 6</span> stay open, with the positions and sources stated.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"14-sources-and-to-check-資料與待查\">13. Sources 資料 <a class=\"up\" href=\"#1-at-a-glance-%E4%B8%80%E8%A6%BD\" title=\"回到摘要 back to the summary\">↑</a></h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Texts and tags: BHSA (ETCBC), OpenGNT 3.3, the chart <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">舊約在新約</span> The Old Testament in the New with OpenBible.info verse votes for section 6, eBible KJV 1769 and ASV 1901 with Strong's tags, OpenHebrewBible and OpenGNT <span lang=\"zh-Hant\">和合本</span> alignments, UBS SDBH and UBS Greek NT dictionaries, ISBE (1915).</li>\n<li>Editions: 和合本; KJV 1769; ASV 1901; BHSA (Hebrew); OpenGNT (Greek NT); LXX = the Apostolic Bible Polyglot (the Sixtine of 1587 as reprinted by Bos 1709, not Rahlfs–Hanhart); Textus Receptus = Scrivener 1894 where it is named.</li>\n<li>The LXX used here (the Apostolic Bible Polyglot) is the Sixtine of 1587 via Bos 1709, not Rahlfs-Hanhart; Daniel there is Theodotion. Every line labelled LXX here is that text. Swete's text is read from the First1KGreek transcription, which has lost words at <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.6.2\">Gen 6:2</span> and <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Deut.32.43\">Deut 32:43</span> in this edition; the reading <span lang=\"grc\">ἄγγελοι τοῦ</span> at <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.6.2\">Gen 6:2</span> and <span lang=\"grc\">ἄγγελοι τοῦ θεοῦ</span> at <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.1.6\">Job 1:6</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.2.1\">2:1</span> were read there. The LXX used here and Swete disagree at <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Gen.6.2\">Gen 6:2</span> and <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.1.6\">Job 1:6</span>; <span class=\"ilref\" tabindex=\"0\" data-q=\"Job.2.1\">2:1</span>.</li>\n\n\n\n\n\n</ul>\n<details class=\"pn across\" open><summary>跨燈 <i lang=\"en\">Across the lamps</i></summary><p class=\"acr\" data-b=\"1\"></p></details>"}});
